ine's Democratic lean plus a favorable midterm environment outweigh Collins's incumbency and fundraising advantages; however, the newest high-quality poll showing Collins ahead and late-breaking ProPublica and FBI coverage keep the edge small and uncertainty elevated.", "watch": "Follow-up surveys will show whether the NYT/Siena Collins lead is an outlier or the start of a shift, and whether the ProPublica and FBI coverage continues to move voters.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Susan Collins (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Maine's Senate race between four-term Republican incumbent Susan Collins and Democrat Troy Jackson is one of the tightest contests of 2026. Polling from August and September averages a Jackson lead of about two points, but the most recent high-quality survey, from NYT/Siena, put Collins ahead by three — her first lead of the cycle — while a large University of New Hampshire poll days earlier had Jackson up four.\n\nFundamentals lean Democratic: Maine sits four points left of the country and the midterm environment favors the out-party, though Collins's incumbency and her $11 million warchest offset that. Expert ratings tilt toward Jackson — four of ten call it a Democratic flip and the rest a tossup — and prediction markets price his chances in the mid-sixties. A ProPublica report and FBI-probe headlines have roiled the race in its final stretch.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7341, "margin": 3.16}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6906, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.626, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6915, "margin": 2.5}]}